# Lyon Gare Part Dieu, a piano & everything

I am currently on my trip back home from a conference week in France and more specifically Lyon, which is that far in the south that we had 25-30 degrees near the end of September - sweet. The trip is about 12-13 hours with only one major change of trains (and a couple near home). That is super convenient since it gives me time to read, relax, code (a good portion of my hobby coding is based on such trips) and looking out of the window (and browse some gem capsules as well as learning Japanese vocab).

Speaking of vocab or the lack thereof. I really learned to love the notion in France to reply to my broken, slow, school-classes dominated language attempts to kindly make me order or phrase my request as polite as I manage to do with an unabated continuous stream of fast French that does not show the slightest sign of stopping or slowing down before the full message was brought accross - not the least affected by my certainly increasingly clueless expression on my face.
I think in fact that this is a great way of learning and showing respect both ways. I have been in countries where there seems to be strong preference to immediately switch to English or even German as soon as my accent was recognized. Btw, it's funny to observe once you pay attention: the most common approach in Germany towards people that show signs of issues with understanding German seemingy is NOT to simply speak slower but LOUDER instead ;-). 

Also, arriving in another country's city via train makes me feel so European which I also enjoy alot. It has nothing of the alieness of being pushed and squeezed through an airplane with all its security and in-line waiting and kerosin burning. Arriving in Lyon - not for the first time -I can't help but notice how relaxed and clean the main station (well, Gare Part Dieu) is. I know very well that there are corners, exits and whole streets where you may not want to be at night and it turns out (a great phrase when you have no idea, but heard rumors) there are places in any large city one might avoid at night, but a railway station isn't optional for a traveller. Also the narrative here in Germany is that you must not have benches in public places like train stations as people will just sleep on it and render them dirty or broken. Not to speak of power plugs and actually comfy seats. Yet, this station has it all. People sit and wait.

No vandalism in my vicinity. Quite the contrary. What is the opposite of vandalism you may ask, well there even is an old fashioned piano somewhere near the heart (awww...) of the station and when I passed it a young man just took a seat there and started playing. I really didn't like myself during taking a picture of that but I think I made up for it by thoroughly enjoying his playing in silence and attention for quite a while.
=> lyon_station.jpg Pianist in Lyon, a pigeon and me listening in awe.
That guy would also not have been out of place at some skater park - or our astronomy conference because it seems that astrophysicists don't feel bound to regulations and society's expectations when it comes to clothing for sure. He played so enthusiastically and beautiful that it really warmed my heart and let my mind take off. I used to play some piano but only at the level of remembering simple tunes (house of rising sun, Chopin's prelude #4, it's my life by Dr. Alban and the Indiana Jones main theme come to mind. I never fully learned an instrument to the level of expressing myself fully. I picked up the harmonica not too long ago hoping that it would buy me some time as it feels like that one could be played at high age, too.

I'm somewhere around mid-life and can see that not all the things I-want-to-do-or-achive-one-day are no-brainers, quite literally. Hence my picking up Japanese (it may not bring me very close to fluency ever but it might prevent dementia which is also valid in my book).

Aside from a book, my laptop, a random mix of clothes, running gear (well, shoes) I took a super wide angle pinhole camera with me in the insane format of 8x10". I limited myself to 2 slides of film and spent quite some free minutes with wondering how to spend those two shots. The focal length (if you can speak of lengths at all here) was so ridiculus that any picture runs danger of becoming artsy fartsy but let's see. It's a slow process, and processing is slow as well.

I am very excited to return home today and see my kids and wife again. Given the delay this train already has because of $something, it will likely become tomorrow but that's not gonna diminish the joy, just delay it - I don't mind delayed presents either for the same reason.
Seeing all these various people walking, running, cycling and scooting (is that a word?) along proudly, bothers, fast & slow, sparkling or grey but all somehow in harmony with life as such ironically (because they were all busy with their own goals and objectives) fed my optimism about humanty's future and that we can do better and surpass this hate, sadness, bitterness and fear that seems to drive (and control) so many people these days.

France, I know you are not perfect, and I like you alot.

